- If you recently changed your name, make sure SSA has your updated name before submitting forms that will be verified.

Common specifics
- Middle name vs initial: If SSA has your full middle name and you only provide an initial (or vice versa), some systems still match; others might flag it. Best practice: match the SSA entry.
- Suffixes (Jr., Sr., III): These are part of the legal name for many verifications — include them exactly as on SSA records.
- Hyphens/apostrophes/spaces: Some databases strip punctuation and still match; don’t rely on that — follow the SSA spelling if possible.
- Forms that ask only for last four digits: Even when only the last four are requested, it’s safer to use the name that matches your SSA/ID records if they’ll use the last four for verification.

What to do now
1. Check your Social Security card (or your SSA online account) to see the exact name spelling on file.  
2. Use that exact spelling on the form whenever possible.  
3. If you have already submitted a form with a different spelling, contact the organization (HR, bank, agency) and tell them there may be a name mismatch so they can advise next steps.  
4. If your name needs to be changed permanently, update your name with the SSA before submitting forms that will be verified.

