Details and practical guidance
- Why exact matching matters: agencies (SSA, IRS, employers, benefits providers) match submitted names to SSA/IRS records. Differences in punctuation, hyphens, middle names/initials, or suffixes (Jr., Sr., III) can trigger a mismatch.
- Typical variations that sometimes pass: omitting a middle name or initial is often tolerated, and using a common shortened first name (e.g., “Mike” vs “Michael”) sometimes works — but you shouldn’t count on it.
- When you must match exactly:
  - If the form specifically says “name as shown on Social Security card,” use the card wording exactly.
  - For tax documents (W-2, 1099) and official government verification, matching SSA records is important.
- If your name on the form is different from SSA:
  - Contact the requester (HR, agency, bank) and ask whether the variation is acceptable.
  - Provide or be ready to provide proof of identity/name (SSN card, passport, driver’s license) if requested.
  - If your SSA record is wrong or you’ve changed your name, update SSA (bring required documents to an SSA office or follow SSA procedures).
- Security note: avoid posting or sharing your full SSN in public forums. Only give it to trusted entities over secure channels. Use only the last four digits when possible.

If you’d like, I can:
- Draft a short message you can send to the organization asking whether the exact SSA spelling is required.
- Review the name on your document (if you paste it here) against the name on your Social Security card and recommend whether to change it.

