Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
Short answer: usually you should use your legal name exactly as it appears on your Social Security record (the name on your Social Security card). Many agencies and electronic verification systems require a close or exact match; small formatting differences sometimes pass, but differences in last name or legal name will often cause a rejection or delay.

A few practical points and steps:

- I noticed a mismatch in what you wrote: the SSN you typed (000-00-9012) ends with 9012, but you also wrote “last four: 1234.” Which is correct? (Avoid posting full SSNs publicly — if you want help, you can just say “my last four are 9012” or similar.)

- Best practice: enter the name exactly as shown on your Social Security card (first, middle, last, suffix). This is the safest option for:
  - IRS/tax forms (W‑2, 1099),  
  - employer I‑9 and payroll setup,  
  - Social Security/SSA verifications,  
  - many background checks and government forms, and  
  - some bank identity checks.

- Minor variations that sometimes pass:
  - Using a middle initial instead of the full middle name (e.g., “Jane A. Doe” vs “Jane Alice Doe”) is often acceptable.
  - Differences in punctuation or spacing (periods in initials, commas) are frequently tolerated by modern systems.
  - Omitting a middle name is often okay.

- Variations that commonly cause problems:
  - Different last name (maiden vs married) or hyphenation differences.
  - Different suffix (Jr., Sr., III) or its placement.
  - Misspellings or use of nicknames instead of the legal name (e.g., “Bob” vs “Robert”).

- If the name on your document doesn’t match the SSA record:
  - If you can, correct the form to match the SSA/legal name.
  - If you legitimately use a different legal name now (marriage, court order), provide supporting documents (marriage certificate, court order, updated ID) and/or ask the requester whether they accept supporting documents.
  - If your Social Security record is out of date, contact SSA to update it (Form SS‑5 or visit your local SSA office). That can prevent repeated verification failures.

- If an online verification fails: contact the organization requesting the SSN to ask what name format they expect and what alternative documentation they will accept (driver’s license, passport, marriage certificate, SSA printout).

If you want, tell me:
- Which name is on your Social Security card and which name appears on the document you’re filling out (don’t post the full SSN), and I’ll suggest the exact wording to use or a short message you can send the requester asking whether the variation is acceptable.

