Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
Short answer: yes — for anything that will be checked against Social Security Administration (SSA) records (tax forms, employment eligibility, benefits, background checks, identity verification), your name should match the name on your Social Security card as exactly as possible.

Details and practical guidance
- Why exactness matters: many organizations (employers, banks, government agencies) match your name + SSN (or last 4) and date of birth against SSA or other databases. Even small differences can cause a mismatch, delay processing, or trigger requests for extra documentation.
- What “match” usually means:
  - Include the same first, middle, and last names or initials as shown on your SSA record.
  - Include suffixes (Jr., Sr., III) if they appear on the SSA record.
  - Hyphens, punctuation, and diacritics are sometimes normalized by databases, but if your SSA card shows a hyphen or space, use it the same way when possible.
  - Nicknames (e.g., “Bob” for “Robert”) or alternate spellings can cause mismatches.
- When small variations are usually okay:
  - Omitting a middle name when the SSA record shows only a middle initial may be tolerated in many systems, but not always.
  - Minor punctuation differences (commas, periods) are often ignored.
- If your name has changed (marriage, divorce, court order): update your SSA record first. Organizations will generally want the SSA name to match the name you give them.

What to do next
- Check your Social Security card (or the SSA’s records) and use that spelling on the form.
- If the organization is asking only for the last four digits of your SSN, still use the last four that match your SSA record — and don’t post or share your full SSN publicly.
- If you get a mismatch or a request for proof, be prepared to provide ID (driver’s license, passport) or a document showing your legal name change.
- To correct the SSA record, you will need to submit proof of identity and proof of the name change (SS-5 form and supporting documents).

Caution about what you shared
- I noticed the SSN you typed includes two different “last four” numbers (you wrote 000-00-9012 and then “last four: 1234”). Please avoid sharing your full SSN in messages. If you want help, just tell me whether you want guidance on “matching the name on the SSA card” or ask me to draft a short message to the organization asking whether an exact match is required.

If you’d like, I can:
- Draft a short inquiry you can send to the organization asking whether the exact SSA spelling is required, or
- Draft the wording for a name-correction request to the SSA (or list the documents you’ll need). Which would be most helpful?

