Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
Short answer: usually yes — you should use the name that matches your Social Security Administration (SSA) record (the name on your SSN card) for any official forms that rely on your SSN. Small formatting differences (capitalization, punctuation, presence/absence of a middle initial) are sometimes tolerated, but mismatches in last name, first name spelling, or missing/extra legal name parts can cause verification problems or delays.

Details and practical steps
- Why it matters
  - Employers, the IRS, and many government agencies verify the name/SSN pair against SSA records. If the name on the form doesn’t match SSA’s record, wage reporting, tax filings, background checks, or identity verification can be rejected or flagged.
  - Banks and other private organizations also verify identity; they may accept minor differences but could ask for proof if something looks off.

- What “must match” typically means
  - Exact legal name as shown on your SSN card is the safest choice (first name, middle name/initial as listed, last name, and any suffix like Jr./III).
  - Minor formatting differences (extra spaces, capitalization, a missing period after an initial, diacritical marks) are often not critical. But differences in spelling, omitted legal last names, or using a nickname instead of your legal first name can cause problems.

- Common scenarios
  - You legally changed your name (marriage, court order): update your name with the SSA first, then use the updated name on forms.
  - You use a different name professionally (nickname): use the legal name for SSN-related forms; you can usually also provide your preferred name elsewhere on the form or as an “also known as.”
  - Middle initial vs. full middle name: usually OK, but if the organization does strict verification, match the SSA record.

- If your name doesn’t match the form you’re filling out
  - Ask the requester what they require (legal name vs. preferred name).
  - Provide supporting documents if they allow (SSA card, driver’s license, marriage certificate).
  - If you need the SSA record updated, contact SSA (in person or via their website) and bring proof of identity and name change documents. Updating can take time, so plan ahead.

- Extra important note about privacy
  - I noticed you included an SSN in your message and an inconsistent “last four” reference. Don’t post your full SSN publicly. If you meant to redact it, please avoid sending the full number here. If you want help that requires identifying which SSN last-four to reference, just say “last four: 9012” (or the correct last four) without revealing the whole number.

If you tell me which type of form or which organization is asking for your SSN (employer payroll, bank account, government benefit, tax form, etc.), I can give more specific guidance and suggest exact wording you can use on the form or in a message to them. I can also help you draft an email to ask the organization whether they require the exact SSA name.

