Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
Short answer: for anything that will be verified against Social Security records (payroll, tax forms, benefits), your name should match exactly the name on your Social Security card/SSA record. For many other forms (bank forms, membership, registrations) small differences are usually tolerated, but it’s safest to use the same spelling/format as SSA.

Details and practical steps

- Why it matters
  - Employers, payroll systems, and government agencies verify your name + SSN against the Social Security Administration (SSA). If the name on the form doesn’t match SSA’s record, verification can fail, causing delays in hiring, tax reporting, benefits, or payments.
- What “match exactly” means
  - Use the same legal name shown on your Social Security card (first, middle, last, and suffix).  
  - Minor formatting differences (capitalization, commas, or periods) usually don’t matter.  
  - Differences that do matter: different last name (maiden vs. married), missing or different middle name/initial when SSA has one, omitted Jr./Sr./III when SSA includes it, or a different legal spelling.
  - Hyphens, spaces, and diacritics can sometimes cause mismatches depending on the system — when in doubt, mirror the SSA card.
- If your name on the document differs from SSA
  - Best option: enter the name exactly as it appears on your Social Security card.  
  - If you’ve legally changed your name but haven’t updated SSA, either:
    - Update your SSA record first (SSA.gov has the requirements and forms), or
    - Explain the difference to the requesting organization and provide supporting documentation (copy of SS card, photo ID, marriage certificate, court order) as they request.
  - If the organization requires the new name immediately (e.g., for payroll), ask their HR or onboarding contact what documentation they accept and whether they can proceed while you update SSA.
- What to provide to verify identity
  - Typical acceptable documents: original or certified copy of Social Security card, U.S. passport or state ID, and legal proof of name change (marriage certificate, court order) if applicable.
- Privacy note
  - Avoid posting your full SSN publicly. I noticed the numbers you supplied don’t match (you wrote “000-00-9012” and “last four: 1234”); please don’t share full SSNs in chats. If you want, give only the last four digits when necessary.
- Want help?
  - I can draft a short note to HR or the organization explaining the name difference and listing the documents you can provide, or walk you through SSA’s name-change process. Which would you prefer?

