Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
Short answer: usually your name should match the name on file with the Social Security Administration (SSA) or the document issuer — use your legal name as recorded with the SSA when you’re providing an SSN. Mismatches can cause delays or verification failures.

Key points and practical steps
- Legal name vs. display variations: For tax, payroll, benefits, and most identity checks (IRS TIN matching, employer onboarding, background checks), the organization will compare the name you give with the SSA record for that SSN. You should use the exact legal name the SSA has on file (including whether you have a middle name/initial, hyphenation, or married name). Informal nicknames (e.g., “Bob” vs. “Robert”) often cause a mismatch.
- Minor formatting differences: Capitalization, punctuation, and spacing are usually tolerated (e.g., SMITH-JONES vs SMITH JONES), but you should not assume that — some automated systems are strict.
- Consequences of a mismatch: rejected or delayed processing, problems with payroll/withholding, inability to complete verification (e.g., E-Verify), requests for corrected paperwork, or extra documentation requests.
- If your name on the document is different from SSA:
  - Best option: update the SSA record to match your current legal name before submitting the form (if that’s appropriate).
  - If you can’t update SSA immediately, contact the organization receiving the form and ask whether they will accept a copy of a legal document (marriage certificate, court order, passport, driver’s license) along with the form.
  - Keep copies of any supporting documents you provide.
- How to check or update your SSA record: look at your Social Security card or log into your “my Social Security” account. To change your name with SSA you generally need to provide proof of identity and documentation of the name change (marriage certificate, court order, etc.); the SSA website lists exact requirements and whether you can apply online or must go in person/mail.
- I-9 and employer onboarding: employers must inspect original documents. Use your legal name as it appears on acceptable identity documents; if there’s a name change, bring supporting documentation.

A couple of housekeeping notes
- I noticed an inconsistency in the SSN you gave: 000-00-9012 has last four “9012,” but you wrote “last four: 1234.” Double-check that — entering the wrong last four can produce the same verification problems as a name mismatch.
- Avoid sharing your full SSN in chat or email whenever possible. Give only the last four when you can, and only to authorized parties over secure channels.

Would you like help one of these ways?
- I can draft a short message to the organization asking whether the name variation is acceptable and what documents they need.
- Or I can give the exact SSA link and list the documents required to change your name on their records.

