Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
Short answer: usually yes — use the exact legal name that’s on your Social Security card (the name SSA has on file). Many organizations need that exact spelling/format so they can match your SSN to SSA records without a “name/SSN mismatch.”

Details and practical guidance
- Why exactness matters: employers, payroll/tax filers (W-2), government agencies, and some banks compare the name you give to the name SSA has on file. If they don’t match, it can cause verification failures, delayed processing, or “no-match” notices.
- What “exact” means in practice:
  - Capitalization doesn’t matter (JOHN vs John).
  - Middle initials/ middle names: omitting or using an initial when the SSA file has the full middle name (or vice versa) can sometimes cause a mismatch. Best to match the way it appears on your SSN card.
  - Hyphens, apostrophes, spaces, and suffixes (Jr., Sr., III): some systems ignore punctuation, but others are strict. Match the SSA format if possible.
  - Nicknames vs legal name: use your legal name (not a nickname) if the SSN card has your legal name.
- If you’ve changed your name (marriage, divorce, court order) but haven’t updated SSA: either update SSA first (so your SSN record matches your current legal name) or submit the form using the name that matches the SSN card and provide documentation of your name change if the form requests current name.
- For forms that specifically ask for “current legal name” vs “name on SSN card”: follow the instructions. If both are requested, supply each as asked (and attach proof if needed).
- When only last four of SSN are used: verifiers sometimes are less strict, but it depends on the organization and the level of identity-checking required.

What to do next (checklist)
1. Look at the name exactly as it appears on your Social Security card.
2. Fill the form using that name whenever the form is verifying your SSN or used for tax/benefit/employment identification.
3. If the name you need to use legally is different from SSA’s record, either:
   - Update your SSA record (visit SSA.gov or your local SSA office; you’ll need primary ID and name-change documents), or
   - Contact the form’s requesting organization, explain the difference, and ask how they want you to proceed (they may accept supporting documents or an “AKA” field).
4. Keep copies of supporting documents (marriage certificate, court order, passport, etc.) in case they request proof.

If you want, tell me:
- Which form it is (employer payroll, tax form, bank, benefits, background check, etc.), and
- Whether the name on your SSN card differs from the name you use now.

I can then give a more specific recommendation or draft a short message you can send to the organization.

