Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
Short answer: in most cases, yes — the name on the form should match the name on your Social Security record (the name on your Social Security card) or the organization may not be able to verify your SSN or may flag a mismatch. How strict they are depends on who’s asking for the SSN.

Details and practical guidance

- Why exactness matters
  - Agencies that verify SSNs (Social Security Administration, IRS, employers using E‑Verify, banks doing KYC) match the name you give against their records. A mismatch can cause delays, a rejection, or a request for documentation.
  - Small formatting differences (capitalization, presence/absence of periods or commas) are usually ignored. Big differences (different last name, dropped/added middle name, spelling errors) are more likely to cause problems.

- Common acceptable vs problematic variations
  - Usually acceptable: capitalization differences; minor punctuation (e.g., “O’Neil” vs “ONEIL”); missing accent marks.
  - Potentially problematic: different last names (maiden vs married), different first name forms (“Michael” vs “Mike” may or may not be accepted), missing or different suffixes (Jr., Sr., III), substantially different spellings (“Jonson” vs “Johnson”), using initials in place of a legal middle name when SSA has the full middle name.
  - Best practice: include the same first, middle (or middle initial) and last name as on your Social Security card / SSA record, including suffixes.

- What to do if your name on the document doesn’t match your SSA record
  1. If possible, enter the name exactly as it appears on your Social Security card.
  2. If the requester requires your current/legal name but your SSA record is outdated (e.g., you changed your name after marriage), either:
     - Update your name with SSA first (visit SSA.gov or local SSA office; Form SS‑5 is used for changes) and provide required proof (ID, marriage certificate, court order), or
     - Ask the requester whether they will accept the name you currently use if you can provide supporting documentation (marriage certificate, court order, driver’s license). Some organizations will accept documentation instead of a matching SSA record.
  3. If the mismatch is a small typo, contact the requester and explain — they may accept a corrected version or ask for proof of identity.
  4. For employers/tax matters: mismatches can affect W‑2/IRS records; it’s usually best to get SSA records updated promptly to avoid issues.

- Practical tips
  - Always check how the requesting organization wants the name formatted (some forms explicitly say “enter name exactly as shown on Social Security card”).
  - Keep copies of documentation used to update SSA.
  - Don’t transmit your full SSN unnecessarily. Use secure channels and only provide the full SSN when required.

If you want, I can:
- Help you draft a short message to the organization asking whether a particular variation is acceptable (tell me how your name currently appears on the document vs on your SSN card), or
- Walk you through what documents you’ll need to change your name with the SSA.

