Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
Quick note before anything else: you posted what looks like a full SSN (000-00-9012) and then said the last four are 1234 — those two don’t match. Also, it’s safer not to share your full SSN in messages. Going forward, give only the last four when asking about forms or verification.

Short answer
- In most cases the name on the form should match your legal name as it appears in the Social Security Administration (SSA) records (the name linked to your SSN). Small differences like capitalization don’t matter, but differences in spelling, missing/extra hyphens, omitted suffixes (Jr./III), or use of a nickname instead of your legal first name often do matter and can cause delays or rejections.

Why it matters (common examples)
- Taxes (W-2, 1099): The IRS matches taxpayer names to SSNs against SSA records. A mismatch can delay processing or trigger a “name/SSN mismatch” that must be fixed before credits/refunds are issued.
- Employment eligibility (I-9): Employers must verify identity and work authorization with documents; the legal name on the ID must match the form.
- Banking / KYC: Banks compare the name you give with government IDs and watchlists—differences can slow account opening.
- Government forms / benefits: Many require the legal name exactly as on SSA records or on your legal ID.

What counts as a problem
- Problematic: different spelling (Jon vs Johnn), missing or extra letters, swapped first/middle, different last name, missing legal suffix, use of a nickname when the SSA record has the full legal first name.
- Usually OK: capitalization differences, presence/absence of periods in initials (J. Smith vs J Smith), and some spacing or punctuation differences are tolerated — but not guaranteed.

What to do next
1. Confirm the correct legal name in SSA records:
   - If you have an SSA online account, check it there. If not, you can apply for a record correction (Form SS‑5) with supporting documents (birth certificate, passport, marriage certificate, court order).
2. Use your legal SSA name on forms that tie to your SSN (tax forms, payroll, benefits).
3. If a form already submitted has a different spelling, contact the organization and tell them you want to correct it — they’ll tell you what proof they need.
4. If you need the name changed with SSA, gather documentation of the legal name change and follow SSA’s process to update the record before submitting forms that require an exact match.
5. When contacting organizations, share only the last four digits of your SSN for identification — don’t email the full number.

If you’d like, I can:
- Draft a short message you can send to the agency/employer asking whether they need the exact SSA name (I’ll avoid including your full SSN).
- Or, if you want, draft the exact wording you should put on the form (legal name vs preferred name).

Which would you prefer? And please confirm whether the last-four you meant to share is 9012 or 1234 (or neither) so I don’t repeat the full number.

